course: everyday
lesson: 520
title: Living in a children's home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some children can't live with their parents for a while, because the parents are too ill, can't cope, or can't keep them safe. Some of those children live in a children's home: an ordinary-looking house with a few children and grown-up staff who look after them in shifts. Shay's key worker, Bríd (his own special staff member), does his hair for school photos and knows he hates mushrooms. But at nine o'clock Bríd goes home, and a different worker comes on. «Bríd's grand,» Shay says («grand» is Irish for fine, good), «but she's not mine, ye know?» He loves her and feels the gap at the same time.
explain: The home has rules: lights out at nine, no phones after eight, signing in and out. «It's like prison, man,» Jordan-Lee moans at school. His mate shrugs. «My mum's the same, bro. Phone in the kitchen at eight or it's gone.» Jordan-Lee laughs. Rules are there to keep children safe, in a home and in a family. Some days are hard: the older kids are loud, and one takes Rian's trainers. Rian tells the staff, and they get them back and sort it out. On Keeley's birthday the staff get a cake with sparklers and everyone sings. Keeley smiles, and keeps glancing at the phone, wishing her mum would call. She doesn't.
explain: A kid at school asks Aaliyah, «Where's your mum and dad?» «I live in a home.» «What, like an orphan?» Aaliyah feels small. An orphan is a child whose parents have both died, and most children in homes have parents who are alive but can't look after them right now. Aaliyah doesn't owe anyone the whole story. «I live in a home, it's alright,» is enough. Destiny shares a room with a girl called Precious. They argue about the light for a week, then become best friends, and they still are.
explain: Luca's mum visits once a month. He waits by the window in his best hoodie. Sometimes she comes and they play Uno; sometimes she doesn't turn up, and Luca goes quiet for days. His key worker sits with him and doesn't say «never mind». Elowen hopes every day that her dad will get better and take her home. This year he doesn't, and her social worker says honestly that he may not be able to for a long time. Elowen keeps hoping, and keeps going to dance class too. Hope and getting on with life can live side by side.
explain: Good things happen too. Koa's carer takes him fishing at the river. He lands a trout and the carer yells, «Sweet as, bro, you caught one!» («sweet as» is New Zealand for great), and Koa is stoked (thrilled). Jolene's social worker visits with forms: «We are looking for a foster family for you.» «What if they don't like me?» Jolene asks. «Then we keep looking till we find the right one, and you get to meet them first.» Maddox gets a letter: «Formal notice: placement review hearing. Notice is hereby given that the matter of the above-named child shall be reviewed on 14 November. The child's wishes and feelings will be sought.» He has no idea what it means. His social worker explains: grown-ups will meet to check that where he lives is still right for him, and he can tell them what he thinks.
fact: Some children can't live with their parents for a while because the parents are ill, can't cope, or can't keep them safe.
fact: A children's home is a house where a few children live, looked after by staff who work in shifts.
fact: A key worker is a staff member who looks out for one child in particular.
fact: Rules in a children's home, like bedtimes and phone times, are there to keep children safe; many families have similar rules.
fact: If something is taken or someone is unkind, children should tell the staff, who are there to help.
fact: An orphan is a child whose parents have died; most children in care have living parents who can't look after them right now.
fact: Children don't have to explain to everyone why they live in a home; a short answer is enough.
fact: Friends made in a home can feel like family.
fact: Parents sometimes miss visits; it's normal to feel let down, and it helps to talk to a key worker.
fact: Some children go home again, some move to foster families, and some stay in care for a long time.
fact: Social workers help children in care, explain letters and plans, and look for foster families.
fact: Official letters about children in care use formal words; a social worker can explain them, and the child's views are asked for.
fact: «Grand» is Irish for fine, «sweet as» is New Zealand slang for great, and «stoked» means thrilled.
exercise: Why might a child live in a children's home? | Their parents can't look after them right now | They were naughty and got sent away by the school | Their parents won a holiday without the children | Every child spends one year in a home at some point
exercise: What is a key worker? | A staff member who looks out for one child | A worker who carries everyone's house keys around | A teacher who comes to the home each morning | The boss of all the children's homes in a town
exercise: Why does Shay say Bríd is «not mine»? | She goes home at night and other staff take over | She doesn't like Shay very much at all | She only works in the home on Christmas Day | She is his real mum but won't tell him the truth
exercise: «Bríd's grand.» What does «grand» mean here? | Fine, good | Very old | A thousand pounds | Very tall
exercise: Why does the home have rules like lights out at nine? | To keep everyone safe and well rested for school | To punish the children for being in care | Because the staff want to watch telly | Because electricity is free after nine
exercise: What did Jordan-Lee's mate say about the rules? | His mum has the same phone rule | Homes are the strictest places on Earth | He'd love to live with no rules at all | Jordan-Lee should break all the rules
exercise: An older kid took Rian's trainers. What did Rian do? | Told the staff, who sorted it | Took someone else's trainers back | Kept quiet and wore his slippers | Ran away from the home that night
exercise: What did the staff do for Keeley's birthday? | Got a cake and everyone sang | Forgot all about it | Gave her a day off school only | Rang her mum to come round
exercise: Did Keeley's mum call on her birthday? | No | Yes, first thing | Yes, at bedtime | She came round instead
exercise: «Like an orphan?» What is an orphan? | A child whose parents have died | Any child who lives in a children's home | A child with a parent abroad | A child with no brothers or sisters
exercise: What can Aaliyah say if she doesn't want to explain? | «I live in a home, it's alright» | Nothing, she has to tell the whole story | «Mind your own business, you nosy idiot» | She has to make up a story about her parents
exercise: What happened with Destiny and her roommate Precious? | They argued, then became best friends | They argued and one of them moved out | They never spoke to each other at all | They became sisters by law that week
exercise: Luca's mum sometimes doesn't turn up. What helps him? | His key worker sitting with him | Being told «never mind, cheer up» | Pretending he doesn't care at all | Staying in his room for a week
exercise: Is Elowen's dad taking her home soon? | Not for a long time, maybe | Yes, next weekend | Yes, on her birthday | He already has
exercise: «Sweet as, bro, you caught one!» What does «sweet as» mean? | Great | Sweet | Quite sour | Be careful
exercise: Jolene asks, «What if they don't like me?» What did her social worker say? | They'd keep looking till they found the right family, and she'd meet them first | That she would have to go to the very first family who said yes, whatever happens | That foster families have to like every child, so there was nothing to worry about | That she would stay in the children's home until she was a grown-up anyway
exercise: Maddox's letter says «placement review hearing». What is it? | Grown-ups checking where he lives is still right for him | A test of how well he can hear in his ears | A meeting to decide if he gets new trainers | A court case to punish him for something
test: «u live in a home?? so ur parents are dead». Right? | No, most kids in homes have living parents | Yes, every child in a home is an orphan | Yes, that's what «home» means | No, they're all on holiday
test: «it's like prison, man». What's more true about the rules? | They keep kids safe, like many family rules | They are a punishment for being in care | They only exist in children's homes | They are there to make kids miserable
test: What would most likely happen if Rian never told the staff about his trainers? | The kid might keep taking his stuff | The trainers would come back by themselves | The staff would know without being told | The older kid would say sorry on his own
test: In a cartoon, a children's home is a scary old building run by a cruel lady. What's more real? | Usually an ordinary house with caring staff | Every home is a scary building with a cruel boss | Children's homes don't exist in real life now | Homes are like hotels where kids do whatever
test: Keeley's mate: «why u sad? u got a cake!». Why might she be sad? | She wished her mum had called | She didn't like the sparklers | She wanted a bigger cake | She was tired from singing
test: Elowen: «my dad's getting better, he'll come for me soon». Her social worker says it may take a long time. What's the wise way? | Keep hoping and keep living her life too | Give up hope completely and forever | Stop going to dance until he comes | Pretend her dad doesn't exist
test: Luca's mum misses a visit. A new worker says «never mind, eh». What would most likely help more? | Sitting with him and letting him be sad | Telling him to cheer up and play a game | Saying his mum doesn't care about him | Promising she'll definitely come next time
test: Maddox: «what does ‹hereby› even mean, am I in trouble??» What's true? | Formal letters use stiff words; he's not in trouble, and his social worker can explain | He must be in big trouble, because court letters only go to naughty children | He should ignore the letter, as children aren't allowed to read their own post | He has to go to court alone and explain himself to a judge without help
test: ★★★ Luca's mum is meant to visit once a month. This year she came 9 times. How many visits did she miss? | 3 | 9 | 12 | 2
test: ★★★ Lights out at 9, phones handed in at 8. Jordan-Lee gets home from football at half past 7. How long can he use his phone? | Half an hour | An hour and a half | One hour | No time at all
test: Koa's carer yells «sweet as!» and Koa is «stoked». How does Koa feel? | Thrilled | Seasick | Scared | Sleepy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reason for which a child may reside in a children's home? | The child's parents are presently unable to care for the child safely | The child has been expelled from school for poor conduct | The child has elected to leave a satisfactory family home | Residence in such a home is compulsory for all children
exam: Identify the meaning of the term «orphan» as established in the lesson. | A child both of whose parents have died | Any child who resides in a children's home | A child who has no brothers or sisters | A child whose parents are employed abroad
exam: Given that a child receives a formal notice of a placement review, ascertain the person best placed to explain its contents. | The child's social worker | The other children in the home | Nobody, since the notice is confidential | A classmate selected at random
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rules of a children's home. | They exist for the safety of the children | They exist as a penalty for entering care | They are unknown in ordinary family homes | They serve solely the convenience of the staff
